{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018oAYfXZsoCwdxfS69sAM1C","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Deep Repository Review — 2026-07-18\n2\t\n3\t**Review target:** `cfd24c4` on `dualplan-routing-fix`  \n4\t**Scope:** the full repository, including source, tests, requirements registries,\n5\tgenerated views, CI, configuration, dependency metadata, rendered dashboard, and\n6\tGit history. Deliberately excluded from content review: `docs/log.md`,\n7\t`docs/archive/**`, and `docs/iteration/**`. Generated OKF/dashboard material was\n8\tassessed through its generators, freshness gates, and rendered output rather\n9\tthan line-by-line as hand-authored prose.\n10\t\n11\t## 0. Unfixed items and why\n12\t\n13\tThe first report snapshot was committed as `a93a021`; the table below records\n14\twhat remains after the subsequent confident-fix pass.\n15\t\n16\t| Finding | Still unfixed because | Required next move |\n17\t|---|---|---|\n18\t| **H-02 — orchestration complexity** | A safe decomposition changes the highest-risk state machines and compatibility surface. Extracting helpers without a state model would merely move complexity around. | File and execute a dedicated high-risk architecture WI: characterize transitions, add a no-new-C901 ratchet, then separate pure decisions from Git/session/integration effects. *(§6: WI-225 + WI-226 filed.)* |\n19\t| **H-03 — no license** | OI-4/WI-097 correctly blocks on the owner's public/private and licensing intent; choosing legal terms is not an engineering default. | Owner decision, then add the selected license/notices and README terms. |\n20\t| **M-04 — 390 px dashboard clipping** | The defect is certain, but fit-to-width versus an explicit scroll affordance is a UX choice requiring a rendered iteration. The render-critique skill forbids redesigning inline during critique. | **WI-219 filed queued** with SR-052/SR-054 links; fix in the generator and rerun all 36 screenshots. |\n21\t| **M-05 — non-atomic requirements** | Splitting ratified SRs changes the traceability spine, evidence links, and attestation history. A mechanical prose split would be dishonest. | Planned spine migration with supersession/evidence preservation. *(§6: WI-229 filed — staged, with an owner-attestation hard stop.)* |\n22\t| **M-06 — run-menu shell arguments** | The intended compatibility contract is ambiguous, and POSIX-shell versus `cmd.exe` quoting makes an unreviewed patch unsafe. | Decide shell-fragment versus data-argument semantics, then implement explicit argv/placeholder behavior with cross-platform tests. *(§6: WI-227 filed.)* |\n23\t| **M-07 — 48 live orphan warnings** | Most warnings are retained reviews/spec evidence. Deleting or globally ignoring them would hide useful history; indexing all of them would bloat navigation. | Define evidence-document taxonomy/indexes and ratchet only newly introduced live orphans. *(§6: WI-228 filed.)* |\n24\t| **L-03 — Git identity inconsistency** | Correcting old authors requires disruptive history rewriting; privacy checking is intentionally off. | Standardize future Git identity; rewrite history only as an explicit release decision. |\n25\t| **L-04 — large test modules** | Splitting tests before production boundaries stabilize would increase fixture/patch duplication. | Split alongside the H-02 production decomposition by behavior boundary. *(§6: folded into WI-226.)* |\n26\t\n27\tConfident fixes completed in this pass: H-01, M-01, M-02, M-03, L-01, and\n28\tL-02. The mobile issue was converted into WI-219 rather than silently left as\n29\tprose-only debt.\n30\t\n31\t## 1. Executive summary\n32\t\n33\tThis is a serious, unusually well-instrumented process-engineering repository.\n34\tIts strongest feature is not any individual script; it is the mutually\n35\treinforcing verification system around the scripts: strict traceability,\n36\tgenerated-view freshness, architectural seam checks, cross-platform tests,\n37\tcoverage enforcement, duplicate detection, privacy checks, and an explicit\n38\tgate model. The baseline G3 run passed all 16 checks with 1,030 tests passing,\n39\t34 skipped, and 90.98% statement coverage. *(Validity re-check, §5: the 34\n40\tskips are a session-PATH artifact — no POSIX `sh` visible, so the\n41\tshell-dependent tests skipped; the canonical environment runs the same\n42\tcollection as 1,061 passed / 3 skipped. Same tests, same green.)* Strict\n43\ttraceability found\n44\tzero orphans or integrity findings across 25 stakeholder needs, 66 system\n45\trequirements, 76 low-level requirements, 76 test cases, 65 interfaces, and five\n46\tcomponents. No committed secret, dependency vulnerability, obvious unsafe\n47\tdeserialization, dynamic execution, or unbounded external input vulnerability\n48\twas found.\n49\t\n50\tThe blunt assessment is that the repository's verification discipline now\n51\texceeds the maintainability of the implementation it protects. Fifty functions\n52\tfail Ruff's default cyclomatic-complexity threshold. The nominal coordinator\n53\tsplit in WI-218 reduced physical concentration but left a 2,125-line dispatch\n54\tmodule with a 698-line, complexity-84 function and a compatibility-heavy entry\n55\tmodule. Several registries and module descriptions still state the retired\n56\tserial architecture. The requirements spine is mechanically consistent but\n57\tsome individual requirements have become multi-thousand-character mini-specs,\n58\twhich is the opposite of atomic, reviewable requirements. These are not cosmetic\n59\tcomplaints: the code is safe to change largely because the tests are excellent,\n60\tnot because the core control flow is easy to understand.\n61\t\n62\tThe most urgent immediately-fixable issue is CI supply-chain hygiene. Every\n63\tGitHub Action is referenced by a mutable major tag and no workflow declares\n64\tleast-privilege token permissions. The most urgent non-code issue is the absent\n65\tlicense: a reusable kit with no license grants outside users no clear right to\n66\tcopy, modify, or redistribute it. That decision is already blocked on owner\n67\tintent and must not be guessed.\n68\t\n69\t### Overall assessment by area\n70\t\n71\t| Area | Assessment | Bottom line |\n72\t|---|---|---|\n73\t| Code quality | **Needs focused refactoring** | Idiomatic stdlib Python and good naming, but extreme orchestration functions dominate cognitive load. |\n74\t| Architecture | **Sound model, overgrown implementation** | The registry/generator/checker layering is coherent; coordinator boundaries remain leaky after the split. |\n75\t| Documentation/prose | **Strong but drifting at edges** | Excellent rationale and operational detail; some authoritative contracts contradict current behavior and many docs are undiscoverable. |\n76\t| Security/robustness | **Good runtime posture, weak CI pinning** | Fail-closed checks and explicit trust boundaries are strong; Action references and shell argument composition need attention. |\n77\t| Performance | **Generally fast except doc staleness** | Core generators complete in about one second; `check_docs --stale` took 150.1 seconds because of N+1 Git subprocesses. |\n78\t| Testing/reliability | **Excellent** | Broad unit/integration coverage, cross-platform CI, 90.98% coverage, and green strict gates. |\n79\t| Dependencies/config | **Lean** | Product scripts are stdlib-only; dev dependencies are small and current enough, but reproducibility wording overstates compatible-range pins. |\n80\t| Standards/Git | **Strong with minor inconsistency** | WI-oriented history and hooks are useful; author identity and commit-subject conventions are not fully uniform. |\n81\t| Legal/accessibility/i18n | **Legal blocker; good a11y foundation** | No license; dashboard has good semantic/theme foundations but a reproducible 390 px clipping defect. i18n is not designed in. |\n82\t\n83\t## 2. Prioritized findings\n84\t\n85\t### Critical\n86\t\n87\tNo critical finding was identified. That is not a claim that the system is\n88\tperfect; it means no observed issue justifies emergency remediation ahead of\n89\tnormal review and testing.\n90\t\n91\t### High\n92\t\n93\t#### H-01 — GitHub Actions are mutable and token permissions are implicit\n94\t\n95\t**Location:** `.github/workflows/test.yml:28-30,56-57`,\n96\t`.github/workflows/canary.yml:19-20`, and\n97\t`project-trajectory/ci/check.yml:34-36,81`\n98\t\n99\t```yaml\n100\t- uses: actions/checkout@v4\n101\t- uses: actions/setup-python@v5\n102\t...\n103\tuses: actions/upload-artifact@v4\n104\t```\n105\t\n106\t**Problem:** every third-party workflow action is selected by a mutable major\n107\ttag. None of the workflows declares a top-level `permissions` block, so the\n108\teffective `GITHUB_TOKEN` authority depends on repository/org defaults. The\n109\treference workflow also teaches downstream adopters this weaker pattern.\n110\t\n111\t**Why it matters:** CI executes on pull-request and release paths and is part of\n112\tthis repository's claimed enforcement floor. A mutable tag is not an immutable\n113\tsupply-chain boundary; implicit token rights make the blast radius dependent on\n114\texternal settings. GitHub's own hardening guidance states that a full commit SHA\n115\tis the only immutable action reference. The current action majors are also\n116\tbehind the current official releases: [checkout 6.0.2](https://github.com/actions/checkout/releases/tag/v6.0.2),\n117\t[setup-python 6.2.0](https://github.com/actions/setup-python/releases/tag/v6.2.0),\n118\tand [upload-artifact 7.0.1](https://github.com/actions/upload-artifact/releases/tag/v7.0.1).\n119\t\n120\t**Suggested improvement:** add `permissions: { contents: read }` at workflow\n121\tscope and pin each official Action to its verified full commit SHA, retaining a\n122\tversion comment for update tooling and human readability. Add a lightweight\n123\ttest that prevents the scaffolded reference workflow from regressing to tags.\n124\tSee [GitHub's secure-use guidance](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/secure-use).\n125\t\n126\t**Disposition:** fixed. Meta and downstream-reference workflows now declare\n127\t`contents: read`, use verified full SHAs for checkout 6.0.2, setup-python 6.2.0,\n128\tand upload-artifact 7.0.1, and a scaffold test guards both properties.\n129\t\n130\t#### H-02 — Core orchestration complexity is beyond maintainable review scale\n131\t\n132\t**Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_dispatch.py:1428`, plus\n133\t`agent_loop.py`, `trace.py`, `bootstrap.py`, `gen_trajectory.py`, and related\n134\torchestrators.\n135\t\n136\t```python\n137\tdef dispatch_run(args, root):\n138\t    # 698 physical lines; cyclomatic complexity 84\n139\t    # (the worker engine path is the module-level _ENGINE sibling, not a\n140\t    #  parameter — corrected in the §5 validity re-check)\n141\t```\n142\t\n143\t**Problem:** an explicit Ruff C901 census reports **50** functions over the\n144\tdefault complexity limit of 10 at the review target (**51 after this review's\n145\town M-01 fix** — the batched `git_commit_lookup` itself exceeds the limit,\n146\twhich sharpens the ratchet recommendation below; §5). The worst are\n147\t`dispatch_run` (84), `trace.analyze` (50), `bootstrap.main` (41), and several\n148\tfunctions at 28-29. Source files reach 3,016 lines (`gen_trajectory.py`),\n149\t2,863 (`agent_loop.py`), 2,125 (`agent_dispatch.py`), 2,002 (`trace.py`), and\n150\t1,863 (`bootstrap.py`) by `wc -l` — the counts this repository's registries\n151\tand logs use (this report originally quoted unlabeled non-blank counts; §5).\n152\tWI-218 created sibling modules, but `agent_loop` still re-exports a large public\n153\tsurface and `agent_dispatch` now contains the dominant state machine.\n154\t\n155\t**Why it matters:** reviewers cannot reliably hold a 698-line state machine in\n156\tworking memory. Branch-specific cleanup, rollback, lock ownership, subprocess\n157\tcontrol, registry mutation, and user-facing outcomes are interleaved. High test\n158\tcoverage reduces regression probability but does not make the code locally\n159\tcomprehensible or the failure modes obvious. No complexity ratchet prevents\n160\tfurther growth.\n161\t\n162\t**Suggested improvement:** treat this as an architectural workstream, not a\n163\tdrive-by extraction. First add characterization tests around dispatch state\n164\ttransitions and a report-only complexity baseline that fails only on new debt.\n165\tThen split pure planning/state-transition logic from Git/worktree effects,\n166\tsession execution, integration, and presentation. Replace flat, loosely shaped\n167\tdictionaries at module boundaries with small typed records (`dataclass` or\n168\t`NamedTuple`, compatible with Python 3.8). Reduce compatibility re-exports only\n169\tthrough a documented deprecation path.\n170\t\n171\t#### H-03 — The repository has no license\n172\t\n173\t**Location:** repository root (no `LICENSE` or equivalent); `docs/open-items.md`\n174\tOI-4 / deferred WI-097.\n175\t\n176\t**Problem:** the project presents itself as a reusable kit intended to be copied\n177\tinto downstream repositories but supplies no license and no public/private\n178\tdistribution declaration.\n179\t\n180\t**Why it matters:** absent a license, external users generally have no explicit\n181\tpermission to copy, modify, or redistribute the work. That directly undermines\n182\tthe stated adoption goal and makes dependency or marketplace distribution\n183\tlegally ambiguous.\n184\t\n185\t**Suggested improvement:** the owner must first decide whether this is private,\n186\tsource-available, or open source. Then add the corresponding license, copyright\n187\tnotice, contribution terms, and a short README licensing section. Do not select\n188\ta license automatically; this is an ownership and policy decision, not a coding\n189\tdefault.\n190\t\n191\t### Medium\n192\t\n193\t#### M-01 — Documentation staleness checking has an N+1 Git-process design\n194\t\n195\t**Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/check_docs.py:488-516`\n196\t\n197\t```python\n198\tdef lookup(path):\n199\t    ...\n200\t    out = subprocess.run(\n201\t        [\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%ct\", \"--\", str(path)],\n202\t        ...,\n203\t    )\n204\t```\n205\t\n206\t**Problem:** `git_commit_lookup` memoizes values but still launches one `git log`\n207\tprocess for every unique document and linked non-document path. On this review's\n208\tbaseline, `doc-navigability` consumed **150.1 seconds** while every generator\n209\tand registry check completed in roughly one second or less.\n210\t\n211\t**Why it matters:** this check is part of the per-commit bar. It consumed about\n212\tone-third of the full 455-second parallel G3 wall time and discourages frequent\n213\tlocal verification. Process startup is especially expensive on Windows, one of\n214\tthe project's explicitly supported platforms.\n215\t\n216\t**Suggested improvement:** obtain last-change epochs for tracked paths in one\n217\tGit history traversal, normalize repository-relative path keys, and serve\n218\tlookups from an in-memory map. Preserve the clean skip when Git is unavailable\n219\tand the `None` result for untracked files. Add unit tests for tracked,\n220\tuntracked, nested, and platform-normalized paths and record an empirical before/\n221\tafter measurement.\n222\t\n223\t**Disposition:** fixed. One newest-first `git log --name-only` traversal now\n224\tpopulates the lookup map; tests pin one-process behavior and tracked/untracked/\n225\tout-of-root semantics. The repository check fell from 150.1 seconds to **2.6\n226\tseconds** on the same Windows checkout.\n227\t\n228\t#### M-02 — Authoritative coordinator documentation still describes deleted behavior\n229\t\n230\t**Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/agent_loop.py:1-10,32-48` and\n231\t`docs/requirements/interfaces.csv:16` (IF-015).\n232\t\n233\t```text\n234\tUnattended coordinator: loop fresh agent driver sessions until done.\n235\t... each resumes from docs/status.md ... until docs/run-state reaches an end state\n236\t```\n237\t\n238\t```csv\n239\tIF-015,...,\"agent_loop.py CLI: --root . resumes from docs/status.md headless ...\"\n240\t```\n241\t\n242\t**Problem:** WI-210 explicitly deleted the legacy serial resume driver and made\n243\ta plain launch the dispatcher. The same module docstring later says the legacy\n244\tdriver is retired, so the file contradicts itself. IF-015 is a registry contract\n245\tand is therefore worse than a stale comment: it advertises an interface that no\n246\tlonger exists.\n247\t\n248\t**Why it matters:** operators and downstream adopters use these surfaces to\n249\tunderstand run-state authority, inputs, and concurrency. Contradictory contracts\n250\tinvite incorrect automation and undermine confidence in the otherwise strict\n251\tarchitecture registry.\n252\t\n253\t**Suggested improvement:** rewrite the module opening around its current three\n254\troles (dispatcher, worker, one-shot interactive session) and update IF-015 to\n255\tstate the actual CLI/locking/preflight contract. Regenerate architecture views\n256\tand keep the behavioral code unchanged.\n257\t\n258\t**Disposition:** fixed. The module opening and operative bullet list now state\n259\tdispatcher/assigned-worker/interactive behavior, and IF-015 records the current\n260\tWI-registry/Git-backed dispatcher, worker flags, preflight, and lock contract.\n261\t\n262\t#### M-03 — Off-spine integrity requirements omit registries the implementation enforces\n263\t\n264\t**Location:** `docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv:3,6` (SR-002/SR-005),\n265\t`docs/requirements/low-level-requirements.csv:6` (LLR-005), and\n266\t`docs/test/test-cases.csv:6` (TC-005).\n267\t\n268\t```text\n269\tSR-002 AC: duplicate/malformed SR/LLR/TC/PB/MOD/PART/ASSET id\n270\tSR-005: optional off-spine registries (PB/MOD/PART/ASSET)\n271\tLLR-005: resolves MOD DelegatedSRs\n272\t```\n273\t\n274\t**Problem:** the implementation and tests distinguish current `REPO` rows from\n275\tlegacy `MOD` rows and also enforce `CMP` and `IF` integrity. The acceptance\n276\tcriteria enumerate the older subset only. The requirements are mechanically\n277\tlinked and Verified, but their prose understates the verified behavior.\n278\t\n279\t**Why it matters:** a traceability system that tolerates semantic drift in its\n280\town requirements can be green while communicating the wrong assurance claim.\n281\tAuditors should not need to infer the current contract from test filenames.\n282\t\n283\t**Suggested improvement:** amend SR-002's complete ID list and describe SR-005/\n284\tLLR-005 as PB/REPO (with legacy MOD compatibility)/PART/ASSET. Clarify TC-005's\n285\tmethod without changing IDs or verification state, then regenerate all derived\n286\tviews and run strict traceability.\n287\t\n288\t**Disposition:** fixed. SR-002 now enumerates all integrity-checked IDs;\n289\tSR-005/LLR-005/TC-005 distinguish current REPO from legacy MOD compatibility.\n290\tStrict traceability and generated-view freshness remain clean.\n291\t\n292\t#### M-04 — The dashboard clips rightmost content at the supported 390 px view\n293\t\n294\t**Location:** rendered `PROJECT_STATE.html`, reproducible in\n295\t`scripts/dashboard-shots/shots/390px-light-sw-full.png` and\n296\t`390px-light-arch-full.png`; source generator in\n297\t`project-trajectory/scripts/gen_trajectory.py`.\n298\t\n299\t**Problem:** the rightmost component in the software view is clipped at default\n300\tzoom, and the architecture icicle exposes only its first columns without a clear\n301\thorizontal-scroll affordance. The container technically uses `overflow:auto`,\n302\tbut the default rendered state fails the repository's own T4 usability rubric\n303\tbecause content appears truncated.\n304\t\n305\t**Why it matters:** mobile users can miss components and relationships while\n306\tbelieving they have seen the whole view. This is an accessibility and truth-of-\n307\tpresentation defect, not merely an aesthetic preference.\n308\t\n309\t**Suggested improvement:** file a dedicated dashboard WI as required by the\n310\trender-critique protocol. Explore an explicit scroll cue/fade, a compact mobile\n311\tlayout, or a fit-to-width overview with detail-on-interaction. Verify the entire\n312\t36-shot width/theme/tab matrix after the change.\n313\t\n314\t**Disposition:** deferred as **WI-219**. The defect and acceptance direction are\n315\trecorded; implementation awaits the dedicated rendered UX iteration.\n316\t\n317\t#### M-05 — Several system requirements have become non-atomic mini-specifications\n318\t\n319\t**Location:** `docs/requirements/system-requirements.csv`, notably SR-044,\n320\tSR-045, SR-060, SR-061, SR-065, and SR-066.\n321\t\n322\t**Problem:** eleven system-requirement rows exceed 2,000 characters; SR-066 is\n323\tabout 3,800 characters. Individual rows combine routing, scoring, fallbacks,\n324\tescalation, provenance, safety behavior, and detailed acceptance criteria.\n325\t\n326\t**Why it matters:** the IDs are technically traceable but not independently\n327\treviewable, implementable, or changeable. A small policy amendment forces a\n328\tlarge requirement and its linked evidence to be re-ratified. This is excessive\n329\tcoupling inside the supposedly atomic spine.\n330\t\n331\t**Suggested improvement:** do a planned spine migration: split genuinely\n332\tindependent obligations into stable new SR/LLR/TC chains, retain parent or\n333\tsupersession links, and update stakeholder-need coverage deliberately. Do not\n334\tmechanically split sentences or renumber existing IDs in a cleanup commit.\n335\t\n336\t#### M-06 — Trailing run-menu arguments are concatenated into a shell command unquoted\n337\t\n338\t**Location:** `project-trajectory/scripts/run_menu.py:107-116`\n339\t\n340\t```python\n341\tfull = command if not extra else command + \" \" + \" \".join(extra)\n342\treturn subprocess.run(full, shell=True).returncode\n343\t```\n344\t\n345\t**Problem:** the configured command is intentionally trusted and needs shell\n346\tsyntax, but separately supplied trailing arguments are appended as raw shell\n347\ttext. Spaces, quotes, redirection, `&`, `;`, pipes, and platform-specific shell\n348\tmetacharacters can change meaning instead of being passed as one argument.\n349\t\n350\t**Why it matters:** this is at least a correctness defect and can become command\n351\tinjection when a wrapper forwards data not authored by the same trusted user.\n352\tThe current documentation collapses two different trust boundaries—declared\n353\tcommand text and invocation arguments—into one.\n354\t\n355\t**Suggested improvement:** define the contract first. Prefer an explicit\n356\tplaceholder/argv mode for commands that accept forwarded arguments while\n357\tretaining shell mode only for fully trusted, self-contained commands. If raw\n358\tshell passthrough remains, document that trailing values are shell fragments,\n359\tnot arguments, and reject its use with untrusted data. Cross-platform quoting\n360\tshould not be guessed with a single `shlex.quote` call because Windows `cmd.exe`\n361\thas different rules.\n362\t\n363\t#### M-07 — Documentation discoverability produces 48 live orphan warnings\n364\t\n365\t**Location:** repository-wide `check_docs.py --root . --stale` output.\n366\t\n367\t**Problem:** after excluding generated report bundles and archive noise, the\n368\tchecker still reports 48 live documents that no scanned document links to. Many\n369\tare historical review/spec artifacts where orphaning may be intentional, but\n370\tthe current policy does not distinguish retained evidence from forgotten\n371\tdocumentation.\n372\t\n373\t**Why it matters:** a warning count this high becomes background noise. New\n374\taccidental orphans are hard to see, and useful specifications may exist without\n375\tan entry path for readers.\n376\t\n377\t**Suggested improvement:** define explicit retained-evidence exclusions or\n378\tindexes, then ratchet on newly introduced live orphans rather than enforcing an\n379\tunrealistic zero immediately. Logs and archives should remain exempt as the\n380\tcurrent design intends.\n381\t\n382\t### Low\n383\t\n384\t#### L-01 — README hard-codes a stale interface count\n385\t\n386\t**Loc
